<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=hearing-aid">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hearing aid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Ear trumpet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A hearing aid is a device that improves hearing by making sound audible to a person with hearing loss. The first hearing aids were ear trumpets or ear horns, created in the 17th century as passive cones that gathered sound and directed it into the ear canal. The movement toward modern devices began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a>, and the first electric hearing aid, the akouphone, was created about 1895 by Miller Reese Hutchison. In 1920 Earl C. Hanson patented the first commercially produced vacuum tube hearing aid, which came to market in October 1921. Fully digital wearable aids came in the early 1980s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hearing-aid.jpg" alt="Hearing aid" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jonas Bergsten · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hearing_aid_20080620.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
